 \begin{abstract}
-The performance of classifiers trained on small data, common in healthcare, can be improved by adding synthetic records to the training data. The synthetic data generation approaches in the literature, however, typically do not address the difficulty of small data: they learn only from the few records available. Large language models (LLMs) bring domain knowledge from pre-training, but their output often lacks records or contains records that do not follow the structure of the real data, with missing, extra or misnamed columns and values of the wrong type or outside the expected ranges, which silently corrupt the training data. In this context, we developed \system, an LLM-based approach whose control layer checks and repairs every response against the structure of the real data, and can also enforce value ranges, so that the requested dataset is delivered intact. \system was tested on ten healthcare datasets with seven LLMs from two providers and compared with thirteen generators. It was the only LLM-based generator whose datasets were always intact, achieved the highest fidelity among the LLM-based generators and the largest median F1-score gain of all, and kept a privacy attack near chance.
+The performance of classifiers trained on small data, common in healthcare, can be improved by adding synthetic records to the training data, provided these do not expose the real patients. However, these synthetic data generation approaches typically do not address the difficulty of small data: they learn only from the few records available. Large language models (LLMs) bring domain knowledge from pre-training, but their output often lacks records or contains records that do not follow the structure of the real data, with missing, extra or misnamed columns and values of the wrong type or outside the expected ranges, which must be detected and corrected before the data can be used. In this context, we developed \system, an LLM-based approach whose control layer checks and repairs every response against the structure of the real data, and can also enforce value ranges, so that every requested record is delivered with the columns and types of the real data. \system was tested on ten healthcare datasets with seven LLMs from two providers and compared with thirteen generators. It was the only LLM-based generator that always delivered every requested record in this form, achieved the highest fidelity among the LLM-based generators and the largest median F1-score gain of all, and kept a privacy attack near chance.
 \end{abstract}
 
