 Many prediction tasks in healthcare, such as diagnosing a disease or anticipating an adverse outcome, have too little data. A rare disease may have only a few dozen diagnosed cases, a single-facility study is limited to the patients it can recruit, and labelling records often requires expert review. Sharing data between facilities could add records, but privacy regulations often restrict it~\cite{rieke2020digitalhealth}, so each facility often has to learn from its own data. We call such datasets \emph{small data}: datasets whose number of records is too low to train a reliable prediction model, a limit that depends on the number of features, the number of classes and the frequency of the outcome~\cite{riley2019binary}. A common remedy for small data, and for a lack of data in general, is synthetic data generation (SDG), which adds to the training set artificial records that follow the distribution of the real ones~\cite{qian2023synthcity}. This paper focuses on SDG for prediction tasks on small healthcare datasets.
 
-SDG is, however, hardest when data are small. Oversampling techniques, such as the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE)~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and deep generative models, such as the conditional tabular generative adversarial network (CTGAN) and the tabular variational autoencoder (TVAE)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, learn the distribution from the same few records they are meant to complement, recombining patterns that already exist in those records, for example by interpolating between two existing patients~\cite[Sec.~2.8]{cover2006elements}. Large language models (LLMs) offer another route. They are pre-trained on large text corpora, which gives them domain knowledge about healthcare variables, such as plausible ranges and typical relations between them, and they can generate new records from a few real examples placed in the prompt, without any training on the target dataset~\cite{borisov2023great,seedat2024cllm}. Their output, however, is text that has to be parsed into records, and nothing guarantees that it forms a dataset: a response may contain fewer records than requested, malformed records, missing or invented columns, or values of the wrong type (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). For data management, an LLM is thus an unreliable data source: like any automatic data producer, its output should satisfy integrity constraints before it is loaded into a training set~\cite{ge2021kamino,rorseth2026recovering,schelter2018deequ,breck2019datavalidation}. Two integrity constraints apply: the dataset must have the configured number of records, and every record must have the columns of the real data with values of the right type. First, the number of synthetic records is a design decision, usually a proportion of the real training set chosen, for example, to balance the classes~\cite{chawla2002smote}; a generator that returns 64\% of them leaves the minority class under-represented, which is the problem augmentation was meant to solve. Second, synthetic records are typically loaded into a training pipeline without inspection, where a missing column or a value such as \texttt{age="sixty"} either stops the job or is silently turned into a missing value. Moreover, datasets of different sizes cannot be compared fairly across generators or runs, nor exchanged between facilities in an agreed format. A generator that violates these constraints thus corrupts the training data without the user noticing. In addition, each request to a commercial LLM has a monetary cost, proportional to the tokens (word fragments) processed, which is rarely reported.
+SDG is, however, hardest when data are small. Oversampling techniques, such as the Synthetic Minority Over-sampling Technique (SMOTE)~\cite{chawla2002smote}, deep generative models, such as the conditional tabular generative adversarial network (CTGAN) and the tabular variational autoencoder (TVAE)~\cite{xu2019ctgan}, and language models fine-tuned on the target data~\cite{borisov2023great,solatorio2023realtabformer} learn only from the few records they are meant to complement. They can therefore only recombine patterns that already exist in those records, for example by interpolating between two existing patients~\cite{chawla2002smote}, and most were designed and evaluated on datasets with tens of thousands of records~\cite{xu2019ctgan,kotelnikov2023tabddpm,zhang2024tabsyn}. Large language models (LLMs) can add what the records lack. Pre-trained on large text corpora, they know plausible ranges of healthcare variables and typical relations between them, and they can generate new records from a few real examples placed in the prompt, without any training on the target dataset~\cite{borisov2023great,seedat2024cllm}. This makes them the natural source of synthetic records when data are small.
 
