+Their output, however, cannot be used directly as data. An LLM returns text that has to be parsed into records, and a response may contain fewer records than requested, missing or invented columns, or values of the wrong type or outside plausible ranges (Figure~\ref{fig:teaser}). Such records cannot be added to a training set as they are. A missing column or a value such as \texttt{age="sixty"} stops the training job or becomes a missing value, and a shortfall defeats the purpose of augmentation: the number of synthetic records is usually chosen to balance the classes~\cite{chawla2002smote}, so a generator that returns 64\% of them leaves the minority class under-represented. Like the output of any automatic data source, these records must satisfy integrity constraints before they are loaded~\cite{ge2021kamino,rorseth2026recovering,schelter2018deequ,breck2019datavalidation}. Approaches that prompt an LLM, such as CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}, focus on prompt design and on selecting the generated records, and constrained decoding, offered by LLM providers, fixes the format of each response but not the number of records; none ensures that the requested dataset is delivered. Finally, because the records describe patients, the synthetic data must not reveal the real records used to generate them, which is the reason for generating data instead of sharing the real data.
+
+The problem addressed in this paper is therefore to generate, with an LLM, synthetic records that improve classifiers trained on small healthcare data, delivered as a dataset with the requested number of records and the columns and types of the real data, and without exposing the real patients. Formally, given the training records $D$ of a small dataset, their columns and types $S$ and the configured number of synthetic records $N$, a configuration $\theta$ of the generator produces a synthetic dataset $G_\theta$. The goal is to find a configuration whose dataset belongs to the set $\mathcal{F}(S,N)$ of datasets with exactly $N$ records that conform to $S$ and that maximizes the gain in classifier performance $\Delta U$, while keeping the distance from the real distribution $L_{\mathrm{fid}}$, the privacy risk $L_{\mathrm{priv}}$ and the cost $\kappa$ low:
 \begin{equation}
 \theta^{\star}\in\operatorname*{arg\,min}_{\theta:\,G_{\theta}\in\mathcal{F}(S,N)}
 \bigl(-\Delta U,\;L_{\mathrm{fid}},\;L_{\mathrm{priv}},\;\kappa\bigr)(\theta).
