 $\mathcal{F}(S,N)$ encodes two integrity constraints, the requested number of records and the columns and types of the real data~\cite{wang1996beyond,batini2009methodologies}, and the objectives are the utility, fidelity and privacy on which synthetic data are evaluated~\cite{qian2023synthcity,lautrup2025syntheval}, together with cost: $\Delta U$ is the change in classifier performance when $G_\theta$ is added to $D$, $L_{\mathrm{fid}}$ the divergence between the real and synthetic distributions, $L_{\mathrm{priv}}$ the success of a membership-inference attack, and $\kappa$ the number of tokens processed; Section~\ref{sec:setup} defines how each is measured. The objectives are kept separate rather than combined with weights, because the right trade-off depends on how the data are used; \system satisfies the constraint by construction and is compared with other generators on the objectives.
 
-Deep generators~\cite{xu2019ctgan,kotelnikov2023tabddpm,zhang2024tabsyn} and language models fine-tuned on the target data~\cite{borisov2023great,solatorio2023realtabformer} must be trained on the scarce records and were designed and evaluated on datasets with tens of thousands of records. Approaches that prompt an LLM directly, such as CLLM~\cite{seedat2024cllm} and EPIC~\cite{kim2024epic}, avoid training, but they focus on prompt design and on selecting the generated records; they do not ensure that the output has the configured number of records and the correct columns and types, and they do not report its cost. Constrained decoding, now offered by LLM providers, fixes the format of each response but not the number of records. Finally, generators are usually compared on a single run, which is unreliable in small data. With 40 test records, one misclassified record changes accuracy by 2.5 percentage points, so two runs of the same generator can differ as much as two generators.
-
 We propose \system (\emph{synthetic data agent}), an SDG system for small healthcare datasets that combines a pre-trained LLM, which proposes new records, with a \emph{control layer}, a deterministic component that checks every LLM response before any of its records enters the synthetic dataset. \system treats generation as a data-ingestion problem: the control layer selects the real records shown to the model, repairs and validates every response against the columns and types of the real data and asks for corrections, requests exactly the missing records, and records the provenance and cost of every request in an audit trail. Like an LLM agent, it alternates actions (requests to the LLM) with observations (checks of their output), hence its name. The control layer is key: the integrity constraints then hold by construction rather than by trust in the LLM, and each healthcare facility can run the system on its own data and obtain a dataset that can be audited like any other data source.
 
