 \textbf{Finding~4.} \system achieves the largest median F1 gain among the generators compared, and the LLM is the setting that most changes the results. Among the LLMs (Table~\ref{tab:backend}), the Claude models give the strongest results in our runs: Claude Sonnet 4.6 reaches the highest fidelity (79.07) and the largest gains ($+0.40$ pp of accuracy, $+2.39$ pp of F1), and all three Claude models exceed the best OpenAI model in F1. This comparison is suggestive rather than conclusive, since the Claude models ran at their default temperature and the OpenAI models at 0.3. At list prices, a synthetic dataset costs \$0.02 (GPT-4o mini) to \$0.49 (Claude Sonnet 4.6). Within OpenAI, GPT-4.1 gives the highest fidelity (75.72) and F1 gain ($+1.09$ pp), while the three stronger OpenAI models preserve correlations less well (62.8 to 64.3, against 72.6 for GPT-4o mini). The control layer delivered every dataset with every LLM, and the differences between them are not significant across six datasets ($p=.572$ and $p=.615$ within OpenAI, $p=.377$ over all seven, for accuracy and F1). With the 60 divisions as blocks, the seven LLMs differ ($p\le.002$), and Claude Sonnet 4.6 gives a higher F1 than each of the others after Holm correction ($p\le.039$).
 
